Identity & Access Management (IAM) – Requirements

1. Identity Provider (IdP)
	•	Keycloak is the central IdP managing authentication and authorization for all participants.
	•	A single realm per environment (e.g., dev, test, prod) hosts all organizations (banks).
	•	Each organization is represented by a group or attribute (tenant_id, org_id, bic) within the realm.
	•	Clients (applications) are registered in Keycloak and authenticate using client_id and client_secret.

⸻

2. Authentication (AuthN)
	•	Authentication is handled via OpenID Connect (OIDC) protocol.
	•	Single Sign-On (SSO) is enabled for Paladin and Validium applications through Keycloak.
	•	Keycloak validates user credentials and issues:
	•	ID Token → contains identity claims (username, roles, org, etc.)
	•	Access Token → used to request access to protected APIs
	•	Tokens are JWT-encoded and signed; validated by services via JWKS endpoint.
	•	Support for federated login (optional) via external IdPs if needed.
	•	Password policy enforcement (complexity, expiration, lockout).

⸻

3. Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)
	•	MFA integrated into OIDC login flow through Keycloak.
	•	Enforced per user/group or globally per realm.
	•	Supported methods:
	•	TOTP codes (Google or Microsoft Authenticator)
	•	Email or SMS verification (requires SMTP/SMS gateway configuration)
	•	Step-up MFA required for sensitive actions or admin roles.

⸻

4. Authorization (AuthZ)
	•	Implement Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) via Keycloak realm roles:
	•	admin → Full access (all tenants, can register new organizations)
	•	auditor → Read-only access to organization data
	•	operator → Read/write access limited to own organization (tenant)
	•	Backend services validate:
	•	JWT signature (via JWKS)
	•	Token expiry, issuer, and audience
	•	Embedded claims (tenant_id, org_id, bic, roles, scopes)
	•	Centralized enforcement ensures consistent and secure access across Paladin and Validium.